<table border="0" cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" bgcolor="#FFA8D3" width="100%"><tr><td align="left">
<font class="storytitle" color="#FFFFFF"><b>Glimmer of hope for music industry?</b></font>
</td></tr></table></td></tr></table>

<table border="0" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" bgcolor="#FFFFFF" width="100%"><tr><td>
<a href="modules.php?name=News&new_topic=14"><img src="images/topics/musica.gif" border="0" Alt="Music" align="right" hspace="10" vspace="10"></a>
<div align=justify><font class="posted" color="#000000"><P>The annual <STRONG>Midem</STRONG> music industry gathering in <STRONG>Cannes</STRONG>, <STRONG>France</STRONG>, has over the last few years been lamenting the possible demise of the industry, thanks to file-sharing networks and piracy. <I><STRONG>In.Tech</STRONG> </I>reporter <STRONG>STEVEN PATRICK</STRONG>, on his annual hunt for free wine, women and song, smelled a sea change at this year's meet.&nbsp; 
<P>Just as the free market was preparing to lower the casket on the music industry, a combination of lawsuits against illegal downloaders, a growing legal online market and rising ringtone sales is giving the industry fresh hope.&nbsp; 
<P>The industry had been declining steadily for the past few years – CD sales fell 2% last year – which it has blamed on increased illegal downloading.&nbsp; 
<P>However, 2004 began with some sense of optimism as album sales rose 18.2% in the first week of the new year, compared to the corresponding period the previous year.&nbsp; 
<P>This optimism was evident at the 38th edition of the world’s largest music festival <STRONG>Midem</STRONG>, which took place on January 25-29 in <STRONG>Cannes</STRONG>.&nbsp; 
<P>“A lot of people were nervous and scared the last couple of years, but now they are beginning to (accept the fact that) the old model is dying,” said prog rock veteran and world music icon <STRONG>Peter Gabriel</STRONG>, who made a brief stop at <STRONG>Midem</STRONG> to announce a new artiste initiative.</P>
<P>“We’ve got a lot of new possibilities and we’re excited about what we can do in this more digital world, and we really want a lot of artistes to explore it,” he said.&nbsp; 
<P><STRONG>EMI</STRONG> (US) senior vice-president of digital development and distribution <STRONG>Ted Cohen</STRONG>, who chaired <STRONG>MidemNet</STRONG>, began by saying that online downloading was still a problem but had become more manageable.&nbsp; 
<P>“We’re turning the corner. There’s a real business in front of this saga. We’re here to franchise the disenfranchised. We want to increase the amount of music available online,” he said.&nbsp; 
<P>Yet one still cannot purchase songs by <STRONG>The Beatles</STRONG> online. <U>Artistes like <STRONG>Madonna</STRONG> and <STRONG>Radiohead</STRONG> won’t allow single song downloads, the latter arguing that its songs are an integral part of a conceptual whole – the album</U>.</P>
<P>“We have to free up more catalogue, making more songs available (online),” said <STRONG>Cohen</STRONG>. “We want to learn how to embrace P2P (peer-to-peer) technology, which is not a bad thing if it is 'monetised' and the artistes get paid. We’re here to talk about the issues, the roadblocks and the opportunities.”&nbsp; 
<P>The music industry is still trying to create an online legal market as illegal downloading subsides in the <STRONG>United States</STRONG>. The reason why it’s taking so long is still the same – a labyrinth of copyright issues.&nbsp; 
<P><STRONG>EMI Music</STRONG> executive vice-president <STRONG>John Rose</STRONG> said, “The challenge we face in moving forward is getting all the rights holders in the chain, not just the record labels.”&nbsp;</P><br><br><b>Note:</b> <i>Published at 0:44am W. Europe Standard Time. Source: <STRONG>TechCentral</STRONG>. </i>

</font></div>
</td></tr></table>


</td></tr></table>


