<font class="storytitle" color="#FFFFFF"><b>Shrireen Ritchie talks as chairwoman of the local Conservative Association.</b></font>
</td></tr></table></td></tr></table>

<table border="0" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" bgcolor="#FFFFFF" width="100%"><tr><td>
<a href="modules.php?name=News&new_topic=31"><img src="images/topics/nonsolomadonnna.gif" border="0" Alt="Non Solo Madonna" align="right" hspace="10" vspace="10"></a>
<div align=justify><font class="posted" color="#000000"><P><FONT face=Arial>Former <STRONG>"Sunday Times"</STRONG> editor <STRONG>Andrew Neil</STRONG> has been handpicked to host an evening of political interviews that will help <STRONG>Conservatives</STRONG> in <STRONG>Kensington</STRONG> find a successor for outgoing <STRONG>MP Michael Portillo</STRONG>.</FONT></P>
<P>The interviews will take part in front of an 800-strong audience, testing the potential candidates' media skills in a high-pressure situation. 
<P>Members of the local <STRONG>Conservative</STRONG> party will vote later that night on who should be put forward at the next election as the <STRONG>Tory</STRONG> candidate for <STRONG>Kensington</STRONG> and <STRONG>Chelsea</STRONG>. 
<P>"It's an acknowledgement of the part the media play in our lives," said <STRONG>Shireen Ritchie</STRONG>, who chairs the <STRONG>Kensington and Chelsea Conservative Association</STRONG>. 
<P>"Every person in public life has to communicate well, particularly a member of parliament. This will be similar to a political interview and will be very helpful for members." 
<P>A camera will record the interviews and display the images on a big screen at the meeting in Kensington Town Hall on February 25. 
<P>"It will be as close to a real interview situation as possible," said <STRONG>Mrs Ritchie</STRONG>, the stepmother of <STRONG>Madonna</STRONG>'s husband, film director <STRONG>Guy Ritchie</STRONG>. 
<P><STRONG>Neil</STRONG> was chosen by local <STRONG>Conservatives</STRONG> because he was a "professional broadcaster who is independent, with no political affiliation", <STRONG>Mrs Ritchie</STRONG> said. 
<P>She said it was a "coincidence" that <STRONG>Mr Portillo</STRONG>, who has been <STRONG>Kensington</STRONG> and <STRONG>Chelsea</STRONG>'s MP since 1999, appears on <STRONG>Neil</STRONG>'s <STRONG>BBC1</STRONG> discussion show <STRONG>This Week</STRONG>. Local party officers and not <STRONG>Mr Portillo</STRONG>, had come up with the idea, she said.</P><br><br><b>Note:</b> <i>Read it first here: Published at 9:35pm W. Europe Standard Time. Source: 
<STRONG>The Guardian</STRONG>.</i>

</font></div>
</td></tr></table>


</td></tr></table>


